From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 11:16:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 09/12] robo(reports): guarantee both charts are fresh + prove
 the delay aggregation

Audit of the two charts (Delay Analysis, Production Rate per Hour). Their
MATH is already deterministic in the current code -- the reports were
redesigned in round 5f2e14a to filter by the real production date (not the
shift's tablet-open date) and to bucket the hourly chart on an absolute
productionDate-based timeline, and round 37a076b removed the last wall clock
from the summary route. The old, drifting behaviour the report describes
("8 slabs/hour now, 5 or 11 hours later, same data") was the pre-redesign
code: it filtered by shift.date and divided by shift open-time measured up to
new Date(), so historical values moved with the clock. That root cause is
already fixed; this round closes the remaining gaps.

1. Stale responses (the one live-code determinism risk). The reports API
   routes did not declare themselves dynamic and the client fetched them
   with the default HTTP cache, so a browser or edge cache could serve an
   earlier computed response -- the same filter reading differently later,
   independent of the maths. Now: summary, hourly and trends all export
   `dynamic = "force-dynamic"` + `revalidate = 0`, and every reports fetch
   passes `cache: "no-store"`. Each view is recomputed from live rows.

2. Delay Analysis aggregation is now a pure, tested helper (delayTypesByCode
   in delayTypes.ts) instead of an inline loop taken on trust. It proves the
   chart matches the Delay Log rows: every code included (no Top-N), minutes
   summed from the stored In/Out-derived durationMinutes, ONE row = ONE event
   even for a multi-Robo delay (the machine is never looked at), longest
   first, deterministic on equal minutes, no clock. Behaviour is identical to
   the loop it replaces.

No schema, DB or chart-UI/design change; the calculations and the existing
determinism tests (roboHourlyProduction, roboDelayTotals) are unchanged.

