From 5ffcaf74aaca69a06e61dc31b5daa646354bbb89 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 12:16:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/12] robo(reports): stop a forward-mis-dated slab shifting
 the hourly chart ~24h
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Batch 1432 (31 Aug, 11:20 → 22:50, same day) drew a full empty NEXT-day
timeline after 22:00–23:00, and 2 of the 6 slabs in that last hour appeared
~24h later instead of being counted in 22:00–23:00. Root cause: the hourly
bucketing let ANY later slab's stored production date advance the day, so two
late slabs wrongly carrying the next day's date (a carry-forward data-entry
slip — the batch never crossed midnight) were placed a whole day ahead, and
the window stretched to cover the phantom day.

Round d541fe7 reconstructed a batch that folded BACKWARD (un-redated
midnight) but still trusted a FORWARD date jump, so it did not fix this.

Fix: the production date now anchors the START day once, from the first slab
(register order = the batch's first). It never advances the day again. The day
advances ONLY when the production sequence shows the times themselves wrapped
past midnight (>12h backward) — the real signal for a crossing, which fires
whether or not the post-midnight slabs were re-dated. So:

- A forward date jump while the time barely moved (mis-dating) is ignored —
  the slab stays in the hour it was actually produced. Batch 1432 now ends at
  22:00–23:00 with all six slabs counted there, no next-day timeline, each
  slab exactly once.
- A genuine cross-midnight batch is unchanged: it advances on the time wrap,
  re-dated or not (every existing case — 4, 11, 12 — still passes).

No wall clock, so historical hours stay stable; UI/style unchanged; the axis
is still the dynamic first-In → last-Out window. Pinned by two new tests: the
exact 1432 scenario and a mid-run forward-misdate.

